WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.234
-Hi, Jessica, how are you?

00:00:02.234 --> 00:00:03.936
-I'm good. How are you?
-I'm doing wonderful.

00:00:03.936 --> 00:00:05.238
It's lovely to see you.

00:00:05.238 --> 00:00:07.373
You know, one of the trickiest
parts of this time

00:00:07.373 --> 00:00:09.676
we're living through is people
not seeing loved ones.

00:00:09.676 --> 00:00:12.879
But you're very lucky.
You're actually quarantining
with your grandmother.

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:16.415
-Yes, my grandmother
was quarantining by herself.

00:00:16.415 --> 00:00:19.018
She started in February.

00:00:19.018 --> 00:00:20.453
She was ahead of the whole

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:22.421
government, you know,
recommendation,

00:00:22.421 --> 00:00:24.056
and she got really lonely.

00:00:24.056 --> 00:00:28.928
So, starting beginning of April,
she drove to my house,

00:00:28.928 --> 00:00:31.664
and we have been quarantining
ever since.

00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:35.335
We're exercising and cooking.
And she's a good hangout buddy.

00:00:35.335 --> 00:00:37.503
-That's pretty great
because not a lot of people,

00:00:37.503 --> 00:00:39.305
you know, in adulthood

00:00:39.305 --> 00:00:41.306
get to spend that much
quality time with a grandparent.

00:00:41.306 --> 00:00:42.608
So, in a way, you guys

00:00:42.608 --> 00:00:45.145
have found
a pretty nice silver lining.

00:00:45.145 --> 00:00:47.113
-Yeah, I mean,
she's a pretty cool grandparent.

00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:51.350
You know, her Instagram handle
is, like, @motorcyclegramma,

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:54.920
so, you know,
she's not your typical grandma.

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.156
-Yeah, that's definitely --
I'm pretty sure that handle

00:00:57.156 --> 00:01:00.493
was available
when she went looking for it.

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:03.162
-She didn't have to fight
for that one.

00:01:03.162 --> 00:01:05.131
It's not "motorcyclegramma1."

00:01:05.131 --> 00:01:06.766
-Right. [ Laughs ]

00:01:06.766 --> 00:01:09.235
So, congratulations
on this movie.

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:13.806
This is a female-led,
female-produced spy thriller

00:01:13.806 --> 00:01:15.308
called "The 355."

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:17.577
And the name
has significance, right?

00:01:17.577 --> 00:01:19.112
-Yeah.

00:01:19.112 --> 00:01:22.615
I learned about 355, the name,
from actually the consultant

00:01:22.615 --> 00:01:24.583
that I'd worked with
on "Zero Dark Thirty,"

00:01:24.583 --> 00:01:27.453
and she told me that 355
was the code name

00:01:27.453 --> 00:01:30.390
for the first female spy during
the American Revolution.

00:01:30.390 --> 00:01:33.192
And no one knows her real name
to this day,

00:01:33.192 --> 00:01:35.862
but that was how
she was acknowledged.

00:01:35.862 --> 00:01:39.265
She was this invisible person
who really,

00:01:39.265 --> 00:01:41.567
you know, helped us --
helped our country.

00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:44.804
So, women today in espionage
in the United States

00:01:44.804 --> 00:01:48.307
use that moniker
as a badge of honor, like, 355.

00:01:48.307 --> 00:01:50.609
The women doing the work
behind the scenes.

00:01:50.609 --> 00:01:52.411
-And this was your idea, right?

00:01:52.411 --> 00:01:56.049
To have a movie like this
that had a bunch of, you know,

00:01:56.049 --> 00:01:58.384
female action stars
doing an espionage thriller.

00:01:58.384 --> 00:02:00.419
It's sort of based on the fact
that you were seeing

00:02:00.419 --> 00:02:02.088
that it was happening
on the other side.

00:02:02.088 --> 00:02:04.190
-Yeah, I had seen so many movies

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:06.893
with, you know,
ensembles with male actors

00:02:06.893 --> 00:02:09.529
that were great films
and great actors.

00:02:09.529 --> 00:02:12.131
But I was wondering, like, why
don't we have movies with women?

00:02:12.131 --> 00:02:13.766
I had known that in real life

00:02:13.766 --> 00:02:16.335
women are at the forefront
of espionage.

00:02:16.335 --> 00:02:20.039
And it kind of, in some sense,
was a wish list of mine.

00:02:20.039 --> 00:02:22.207
Because I got to work
with Penelope Cruz

00:02:22.207 --> 00:02:24.510
and Lupita Nyong'o
and Diane Kruger

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.146
and Fan Bingbing and
these incredible actresses

00:02:27.146 --> 00:02:30.216
that, you know, in the past, our
industry hasn't been so great

00:02:30.216 --> 00:02:34.253
at allowing more than one woman
on a set at the same time.

00:02:34.253 --> 00:02:36.189
They don't want us
in the same room.

00:02:36.189 --> 00:02:38.357
So, yeah, I wanted to do it,

00:02:38.357 --> 00:02:41.427
and I guess
I kind of made it happen.

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:44.664
-You, I guess, presented
the idea that a film like this

00:02:44.664 --> 00:02:47.166
would necessarily
then need a Bond Boy,

00:02:47.166 --> 00:02:48.934
the sort of alternative
to the Bond Girls that

00:02:48.934 --> 00:02:51.503
we've gotten used to over
the last, I guess, 50 years.

00:02:51.503 --> 00:02:53.105
And was that --

00:02:53.105 --> 00:02:57.042
How were you greeted
with the idea of the Bond Boy?

00:02:57.042 --> 00:02:58.711
-Well, you know, I had realized

00:02:58.711 --> 00:03:01.547
that men have been discriminated
against for so long

00:03:01.547 --> 00:03:04.484
because they had only been
known for their skills

00:03:04.484 --> 00:03:06.886
and their talent
and their knowledge

00:03:06.886 --> 00:03:08.354
and the things that they say,

00:03:08.354 --> 00:03:11.490
and they were not acknowledged
for their sexual desirability

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:13.526
in the way that women have had.

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:15.127
So I thought, you know what?

00:03:15.127 --> 00:03:17.530
It's about time
we do men justice

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:18.998
and let's have some Bond Boys.

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:21.500
So, I just said it as a tweet,
as a joke,

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:24.536
and the funny thing is
I actually received requests

00:03:24.536 --> 00:03:27.740
and submissions from people
to be Bond Boys.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:30.476
-That's -- I mean,
that says a lot because

00:03:30.476 --> 00:03:32.178
based on the history
of Bond Girls,

00:03:32.178 --> 00:03:33.680
you're really
on the chopping block

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.281
as far as your character's,

00:03:35.281 --> 00:03:37.416
you know, ability
to survive a whole film.

00:03:37.416 --> 00:03:38.984
But you got some pretty --

00:03:38.984 --> 00:03:40.720
You got some really nice hunks
in this film.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.455
I tip my cap.
-Yeah. I will say.

00:03:43.455 --> 00:03:44.691
And also, too, it's time

00:03:44.691 --> 00:03:46.792
that the guys
get to be eye candy, too.

00:03:46.792 --> 00:03:47.993
-Yeah. Why not?

00:03:47.993 --> 00:03:49.762
You know, they're the ones
that are floating

00:03:49.762 --> 00:03:52.131
in the middle of a pool
and somebody goes, "Whoa."

00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:54.500
-Well, I don't want
to give too much away,

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.535
but I will say
that not all of them

00:03:56.535 --> 00:03:59.772
have a successful journey
in our film.

00:03:59.772 --> 00:04:01.941
-Hey, such is the life
of a Bond Boy.

00:04:01.941 --> 00:04:04.210
You know what?
You get what you sign up for.

00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:05.711
We actually have a trailer.

00:04:05.711 --> 00:04:07.680
You know, we didn't show a clip
on the way in because we have

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.849
something a little more
substantial to show.

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:10.883
So let's take a quick look.

00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:12.651
-The threat's
not on the horizon.

00:04:12.651 --> 00:04:15.087
It's here. Right now.

00:04:15.087 --> 00:04:17.957
We're the only ones that
can prevent World War III.

00:04:17.957 --> 00:04:20.826
I'm putting together a team, and
you're the best at what you do.

00:04:20.826 --> 00:04:23.696
-We all work for different
intelligence agencies --

00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:28.668
American, British, Colombian,
German, Chinese.

00:04:28.668 --> 00:04:30.102
♪♪

00:04:30.102 --> 00:04:32.271
But now we need to join forces.

00:04:32.271 --> 00:04:33.873
-They have an army.

00:04:33.873 --> 00:04:36.308
-What do we have? And please
don't say each other.

00:04:36.308 --> 00:04:38.844
-Never.

00:04:38.844 --> 00:04:40.113
-I'm scared.
-Good.

00:04:40.113 --> 00:04:41.981
Only an idiot isn't scared.

00:04:41.981 --> 00:04:44.917
♪♪

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:47.319
-We're The 355.

00:04:47.319 --> 00:04:49.388
-I like the new team.
-We'll take it from here.

00:04:49.388 --> 00:04:51.057
♪♪

00:04:51.057 --> 00:04:54.126
-I'm very pro anything that has
the line "joining forces."

00:04:54.126 --> 00:04:55.928
I like that a great deal.

00:04:55.928 --> 00:04:59.432
-A lot of great movies
have had that line.

00:04:59.432 --> 00:05:01.734
Rival agents joining forces.
-Right.

00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:03.435
You're watching the trailer
saying,

00:05:03.435 --> 00:05:05.838
"I hope they can make it work."

00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:09.942
You had to return to production.
Was it in London?

00:05:09.942 --> 00:05:12.511
Is that where you went back
to finish up filming?

00:05:12.511 --> 00:05:13.812
-Yeah, that's where we --

00:05:13.812 --> 00:05:15.782
That was our base
for when we shot the film.

00:05:15.782 --> 00:05:19.285
And then we did some additional
scenes right in the middle

00:05:19.285 --> 00:05:21.854
of this whole 2020 situation
we're in.

00:05:21.854 --> 00:05:26.024
So in July, we went,
and it was quite an ordeal.

00:05:26.024 --> 00:05:27.693
You know, testing all the time.

00:05:27.693 --> 00:05:30.329
We didn't have any cases.
Thank goodness.

00:05:30.329 --> 00:05:32.765
We were very, very careful
and very safe.

00:05:32.765 --> 00:05:35.534
And so I guess it proved
that it can be done.

00:05:35.534 --> 00:05:37.703
-One of the steps
you had to take personally

00:05:37.703 --> 00:05:39.372
was the self test.

00:05:39.372 --> 00:05:42.008
I have also -- You know, we have
protocols in place here.

00:05:42.008 --> 00:05:45.878
And it's a mix of being tested
and then also the self-swabbing.

00:05:45.878 --> 00:05:49.015
How do you feel like you did
as a self-swabber?

00:05:49.015 --> 00:05:50.849
-Well, in the beginning,
I was terrible.

00:05:50.849 --> 00:05:52.218
Because, you know, you think --

00:05:52.218 --> 00:05:54.186
You see all the --
hear the horror stories

00:05:54.186 --> 00:05:56.188
about it has to go
all the way up your nose.

00:05:56.188 --> 00:05:58.357
And I was like, "This is
just not going to work."

00:05:58.357 --> 00:06:01.094
And I was always crying.
And then I just got used it.

00:06:01.094 --> 00:06:02.895
And the instructions say --

00:06:02.895 --> 00:06:05.898
Just, you know, you stop when
you feel a gentle resistance.

00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:08.935
As soon as I realized that, I
was like, "This is super easy."

00:06:08.935 --> 00:06:10.269
And I still do it, actually,

00:06:10.269 --> 00:06:12.305
because quarantining
with my grandmother,

00:06:12.305 --> 00:06:13.973
I want to make sure that there's

00:06:13.973 --> 00:06:15.607
a safe environment
that we're in.

00:06:15.607 --> 00:06:17.610
I want to be responsible
to the people around me.

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:19.545
So I test myself regularly.

00:06:19.545 --> 00:06:22.448
-A couple times a week, I am
doing the self-swab, as well.

00:06:22.448 --> 00:06:25.184
And I can't believe
I've reached this point.

00:06:25.184 --> 00:06:27.620
And it's really depressing
to be in a place where I'm like,

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:29.389
"I think I'm really good
at this now.

00:06:29.389 --> 00:06:31.257
I think I'm a really good
self-swabber."

00:06:31.257 --> 00:06:34.060
-"I am so good
at putting things up my nose."

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:36.929
-It's also really fun to think
of someone who's in that trailer

00:06:36.929 --> 00:06:39.999
being like, "Uh, I don't want
to put anything up my nose!"

00:06:39.999 --> 00:06:43.469
-We actually did a --
I will say --

00:06:43.469 --> 00:06:45.905
I'm sure you're really good
at it, but I did hear

00:06:45.905 --> 00:06:48.774
that there were a lot
of big babies on our set,

00:06:48.774 --> 00:06:52.311
and some of the biggest babies
have the biggest muscles.

00:06:52.311 --> 00:06:54.447
-[ Laughs ]

00:06:54.447 --> 00:06:59.351
-Then I meant to say
I was a baby about it.

00:06:59.351 --> 00:07:02.054
So great to see you
as always, Jessica.

00:07:02.054 --> 00:07:04.122
I really look forward
to the film.

00:07:04.122 --> 00:07:06.758
And hopefully next time
we can see you in person.

00:07:06.758 --> 00:07:09.395
-I know. I look forward
to being in a room together.

00:07:09.395 --> 00:07:13.332
And let's finish off this year
in a great place.

00:07:13.332 --> 00:07:15.701
Nowhere to go but up, right?
-That's sounds like

00:07:15.701 --> 00:07:18.805
a very accurate framing
of the situation we're in.

00:07:18.805 --> 00:07:21.040
-Yes.

